1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:02,626
Last time, a ball launched at twenty-five metres

2
00:00:02,666 --> 00:00:05,973
per second, at fifty degrees, landed sixty-two point

3
00:00:06,073 --> 00:00:06,895
eight metres away.

4
00:00:07,578 --> 00:00:10,191
At forty degrees, it landed in exactly the

5
00:00:10,233 --> 00:00:10,735
same spot.

6
00:00:11,538 --> 00:00:13,762
Forty and fifty add up to ninety, and

7
00:00:13,843 --> 00:00:15,746
two angles that add to ninety degrees are

8
00:00:15,767 --> 00:00:17,250
called complementary angles.

9
00:00:17,933 --> 00:00:19,313
But the fifty degree ball was in the

10
00:00:19,394 --> 00:00:21,575
air for about three point nine one seconds,

11
00:00:21,996 --> 00:00:23,756
and the forty degree ball for only three

12
00:00:23,797 --> 00:00:24,357
point two eight.

13
00:00:24,978 --> 00:00:26,731
So how can they possibly land in the

14
00:00:26,773 --> 00:00:27,356
same place?

15
00:00:27,986 --> 00:00:28,414
Have a think.

16
00:00:31,981 --> 00:00:34,049
Let's build the range from the two columns.

17
00:00:34,631 --> 00:00:36,675
The flight time comes from the vertical column:

18
00:00:37,037 --> 00:00:39,382
two u sin theta over g, from last

19
00:00:39,423 --> 00:00:39,622
time.

20
00:00:40,385 --> 00:00:43,026
The horizontal column turns that time into a

21
00:00:43,147 --> 00:00:43,546
distance,

22
00:00:44,348 --> 00:00:44,987
so the range,

23
00:00:45,529 --> 00:00:47,349
R, is u x times T.

24
00:00:48,071 --> 00:00:50,755
That's u cos theta, times two u sin

25
00:00:50,796 --> 00:00:53,280
theta over g, which is two u squared

26
00:00:53,321 --> 00:00:55,144
sin theta cos theta over g.

27
00:00:56,127 --> 00:00:58,812
So the range depends on both components multiplied

28
00:00:58,833 --> 00:01:00,735
together: how fast the ball crosses,

29
00:01:01,258 --> 00:01:02,579
and how long it stays up.

30
00:01:03,048 --> 00:01:05,040
Now look at the components for fifty and

31
00:01:05,142 --> 00:01:05,845
forty degrees.

32
00:01:06,670 --> 00:01:09,478
At fifty degrees, the ball crosses at sixteen

33
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:11,986
point one metres per second and goes up

34
00:01:12,208 --> 00:01:13,471
at nineteen point two.

35
00:01:14,295 --> 00:01:16,421
At forty degrees, it's the other way round:

36
00:01:16,943 --> 00:01:19,952
nineteen point two across, and sixteen point one

37
00:01:20,154 --> 00:01:20,233
up.

38
00:01:21,077 --> 00:01:24,584
Complementary angles swap the two components, because cos

39
00:01:24,625 --> 00:01:26,488
fifty is sin forty.

40
00:01:27,191 --> 00:01:29,938
The steeper launch stays up longer, but crosses

41
00:01:30,039 --> 00:01:30,760
more slowly.

42
00:01:31,444 --> 00:01:34,318
The flatter launch crosses faster, for a shorter

43
00:01:34,379 --> 00:01:34,660
time.

44
00:01:35,666 --> 00:01:38,557
Swapping two numbers doesn't change their product, so

45
00:01:38,599 --> 00:01:40,185
the ranges match exactly.

46
00:01:40,781 --> 00:01:42,670
There's a neater way to write the range.

47
00:01:43,412 --> 00:01:45,879
Two sin theta cos theta is the same

48
00:01:46,019 --> 00:01:47,302
as sin of two theta.

49
00:01:48,165 --> 00:01:50,576
That's a trig identity, called the double angle

50
00:01:50,617 --> 00:01:52,124
formula, and you can check it.

51
00:01:52,748 --> 00:01:55,693
Two times sin thirty times cos thirty is

52
00:01:55,773 --> 00:01:57,417
zero point eight six six,

53
00:01:57,978 --> 00:01:59,060
and so is sin sixty.

54
00:01:59,762 --> 00:02:02,919
So R equals u squared sin two theta

55
00:02:03,202 --> 00:02:03,643
over g.

56
00:02:04,246 --> 00:02:06,288
But this shortcut only works when the ball

57
00:02:06,329 --> 00:02:08,292
lands at the same level it was launched

58
00:02:08,353 --> 00:02:10,876
from, with no air resistance, because it was

59
00:02:10,977 --> 00:02:12,939
built from the level-ground flight time.

60
00:02:13,408 --> 00:02:16,264
This form also shows which angle goes furthest.

61
00:02:17,169 --> 00:02:19,049
Sin two theta can never be bigger than

62
00:02:19,251 --> 00:02:21,751
one, and it equals one when two theta

63
00:02:21,872 --> 00:02:24,873
is ninety degrees, so when theta is forty-five

64
00:02:24,914 --> 00:02:25,234
degrees.

65
00:02:25,975 --> 00:02:28,401
Then the two components are equal, and the

66
00:02:28,462 --> 00:02:30,326
range is u squared over g.

67
00:02:31,069 --> 00:02:33,316
On this graph of range against angle, the

68
00:02:33,337 --> 00:02:36,145
curve rises to its peak at forty-five, then

69
00:02:36,307 --> 00:02:37,490
falls away symmetrically.

70
00:02:38,033 --> 00:02:41,280
So every angle below forty-five has a partner

71
00:02:41,522 --> 00:02:42,804
above it, at the same height.

72
00:02:43,286 --> 00:02:44,603
That's the complementary pair.

73
00:02:45,149 --> 00:02:46,034
Let's check with numbers.

74
00:02:46,740 --> 00:02:48,710
A ball is launched from level ground at

75
00:02:48,831 --> 00:02:50,218
fifteen metres per second.

76
00:02:50,922 --> 00:02:51,764
First, the conditions:

77
00:02:52,488 --> 00:02:54,594
same launch and landing level, and no air

78
00:02:54,636 --> 00:02:56,361
resistance, so the shortcut is allowed.

79
00:02:57,184 --> 00:02:57,924
At thirty degrees,

80
00:02:58,486 --> 00:03:02,031
R is fifteen squared times sin sixty, over

81
00:03:02,112 --> 00:03:04,776
nine point eight, about nineteen point nine metres.

82
00:03:05,578 --> 00:03:09,019
At forty-five degrees, sin ninety is one, so

83
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:11,220
R is two hundred and twenty-five over nine

84
00:03:11,261 --> 00:03:13,682
point eight, about twenty-three point zero metres.

85
00:03:14,504 --> 00:03:16,811
At sixty degrees, sin one hundred and twenty

86
00:03:17,052 --> 00:03:20,141
equals sin sixty, so it's nineteen point nine

87
00:03:20,162 --> 00:03:20,642
metres again.

88
00:03:22,201 --> 00:03:25,187
Now twenty-five metres per second, at twenty-five and

89
00:03:25,247 --> 00:03:26,129
sixty-five degrees,

90
00:03:26,670 --> 00:03:27,853
another complementary pair.

91
00:03:28,595 --> 00:03:31,303
Both ranges come out at about forty-eight point

92
00:03:31,343 --> 00:03:31,864
nine metres.

93
00:03:32,426 --> 00:03:34,055
But the flights are very different.

94
00:03:34,897 --> 00:03:37,519
The flight time is two u sin theta

95
00:03:37,679 --> 00:03:38,119
over g,

96
00:03:38,899 --> 00:03:41,061
so the sixty-five degree ball stays up for

97
00:03:41,161 --> 00:03:43,422
about four point six two seconds, more than

98
00:03:43,522 --> 00:03:45,983
twice the twenty-five degree ball's two point one

99
00:03:46,043 --> 00:03:46,564
six seconds.

100
00:03:47,386 --> 00:03:48,841
It also climbs much higher.

101
00:03:49,568 --> 00:03:51,816
The same range doesn't mean the same motion.

102
00:03:52,359 --> 00:03:54,692
It means the extra time is exactly cancelled

103
00:03:54,733 --> 00:03:55,738
by the slower crossing.

104
00:03:56,348 --> 00:03:57,434
Two traps to avoid.

105
00:03:58,098 --> 00:04:01,065
First, the forty-five degree rule and the complementary

106
00:04:01,127 --> 00:04:03,131
pairs are level-ground results.

107
00:04:03,853 --> 00:04:05,594
If the ball lands lower than it started,

108
00:04:06,155 --> 00:04:08,796
from a cliff for example, they fail, and

109
00:04:08,917 --> 00:04:09,557
that's next time.

110
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:13,266
Second, a steeper launch doesn't always go shorter,

111
00:04:13,587 --> 00:04:14,829
or always go further.

112
00:04:15,552 --> 00:04:18,018
The range rises up to forty-five degrees and

113
00:04:18,059 --> 00:04:18,719
then falls,

114
00:04:19,322 --> 00:04:20,965
and that's exactly why the pairs exist.

115
00:04:21,621 --> 00:04:22,605
Here's a way to remember it:

116
00:04:23,127 --> 00:04:24,914
complements swap the components.

117
00:04:25,416 --> 00:04:28,345
Ninety minus theta swaps u x and u

118
00:04:28,646 --> 00:04:31,053
y, and on level ground the range depends

119
00:04:31,093 --> 00:04:33,159
on their product, so it stays the same.

120
00:04:35,742 --> 00:04:36,185
Your turn.

121
00:04:37,291 --> 00:04:39,678
A ball is launched from level ground at

122
00:04:39,779 --> 00:04:41,684
fourteen point zero metres per second.

123
00:04:42,306 --> 00:04:45,169
Calculate its range when it's launched at forty-five

124
00:04:45,209 --> 00:04:45,570
degrees,

125
00:04:46,090 --> 00:04:48,132
and when it's launched at twenty degrees.

126
00:04:48,773 --> 00:04:51,056
Then state another launch angle that gives the

127
00:04:51,136 --> 00:04:52,917
same range as twenty degrees.

128
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:54,694
Pause here to answer.

129
00:05:05,468 --> 00:05:07,476
It's level ground, so the shortcut works.

130
00:05:08,119 --> 00:05:09,099
At forty-five degrees, R

131
00:05:09,861 --> 00:05:12,782
is fourteen squared over nine point eight, twenty

132
00:05:12,823 --> 00:05:13,543
point zero metres.

133
00:05:14,204 --> 00:05:17,446
At twenty degrees, it's fourteen squared times sin

134
00:05:17,547 --> 00:05:19,327
forty, over nine point eight,

135
00:05:19,829 --> 00:05:21,089
about twelve point nine metres.

136
00:05:21,751 --> 00:05:24,288
The matching angle is the complement, seventy degrees.

137
00:05:25,114 --> 00:05:27,742
Using sin twenty instead of sin forty is

138
00:05:27,783 --> 00:05:28,524
the slip to avoid.

139
00:05:30,377 --> 00:05:30,779
Last one.

140
00:05:31,485 --> 00:05:33,826
Two projectiles are launched from level ground at

141
00:05:33,868 --> 00:05:34,707
the same speed,

142
00:05:35,249 --> 00:05:37,450
one at thirty degrees and one at sixty

143
00:05:37,471 --> 00:05:37,791
degrees.

144
00:05:38,432 --> 00:05:41,294
Compare their ranges, their times of flight and

145
00:05:41,335 --> 00:05:42,436
their maximum heights,

146
00:05:42,977 --> 00:05:45,539
and explain why the ranges are equal even

147
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:47,161
though the flight times are not.

148
00:05:47,925 --> 00:05:49,071
Pause here to answer.

149
00:05:59,801 --> 00:06:02,851
Thirty and sixty are complementary, so the ranges

150
00:06:02,891 --> 00:06:03,233
are equal.

151
00:06:03,814 --> 00:06:06,599
Flight time depends on sin theta, so the

152
00:06:06,639 --> 00:06:09,064
sixty degree one stays up about one point

153
00:06:09,144 --> 00:06:10,467
seven three times as long.

154
00:06:11,209 --> 00:06:13,802
Height depends on sin squared theta, so it

155
00:06:13,842 --> 00:06:15,350
climbs three times as high.

156
00:06:16,012 --> 00:06:18,719
The ranges still match because its horizontal component

157
00:06:18,819 --> 00:06:21,045
is smaller by exactly the same factor.

158
00:06:21,788 --> 00:06:24,816
Saying equal ranges mean equal flight times is

159
00:06:24,858 --> 00:06:25,559
the classic slip.

160
00:06:27,221 --> 00:06:29,725
So on level ground, the range depends on

161
00:06:29,946 --> 00:06:30,827
u x times u

162
00:06:31,448 --> 00:06:34,213
y, which is why complementary angles tie and

163
00:06:34,313 --> 00:06:35,576
forty-five degrees wins.

164
00:06:36,277 --> 00:06:38,587
But all of that came from the level-ground

165
00:06:38,707 --> 00:06:39,229
flight time.

166
00:06:39,992 --> 00:06:41,516
Now put the launcher on top of a

167
00:06:41,636 --> 00:06:44,162
thirty metre cliff, firing at twenty metres per

168
00:06:44,182 --> 00:06:44,463
second.

169
00:06:45,146 --> 00:06:46,974
The ball falls below its launch level.

170
00:06:47,597 --> 00:06:50,028
Do thirty and sixty degrees still land together,

171
00:06:50,652 --> 00:06:52,399
and does forty-five still go furthest?

